1: # Ordinary III and IV caps in the three primitive source profiles
2: 
3: Working proof for independent audit, 2026-11-17. This file constructs
4: the actual six one-cap sectors and computes every integral gluing
5: map. It assigns no parameter-return index. The positive parameter
6: family is retained as lambda=r^m; the source root alone does not
7: select its return. Existing manuscript TeX is unchanged.
8: 
9: ## OA.1. Sources, root domains and the exact excluded norm
10: 
11: Use the marked rational elliptic sources and their actual translations
12: from P3K.22. The six data are as follows. A chosen I1 is the sole
13: simple whole-fibre zero of the suspension linearization, and the
14: displayed additive site is the only logarithmic cap.
15: 
16: | Source profile | Capped fibre | (a,b) in M=O(aG-O), Q=bG | Allowed root |
17: |---|---|---|---|
18: | I7+III+2I1 | III | (14,1) | m=4l+1, l>=0, reduced III |
19: | I7+III+2I1 | III | (7,2) | m=4l, l>=1, good |
20: | I5+I3+III+I1 | III | (30,1) | m=4l+1, l>=0, reduced III |
21: | I5+I3+III+I1 | III | (15,2) | m=4l, l>=1, good |
22: | I5+IV+I2+I1 | IV | (30,1) | m=3l+1, l>=0, reduced IV |
23: | I5+IV+I2+I1 | IV | (10,3) | m=3l, l>=1, good |
24: 
25: Write p=2 at III and p=3 at IV. At every retained reducible fibre,
26: Q permutes components transitively and aG meets the identity
27: component. At the capped fibre, a is divisible by p in the reduced
28: case; in the good case b=p and a is prime to p. These assertions
29: follow in the marked basis from (N,k)=(7,2),(2,1) and
30: (5,1),(3,1),(2,1), respectively. The exact integral matrices below
31: also retain these actions. The whole-fibre Hom line has degree
32: ab*h(G)=1. Thus its chosen scalar section has precisely the stated
33: simple zero; no component-supported zero is introduced.
34: 
35: The norm obstruction for the complementary disallowed good roots
36: is an equation on the actual central elliptic curve. The canonical
37: good-root deck has linear part E of order four or three. An affine
38: source lift has the form t_R E. Since r divides m and E has no
39: eigenvalue one,
40: 
41:     (t_R E)^m = t_{(1+E+...+E^(m-1))R} = identity.
42: 
43: The endomorphism sum is zero: its product with E-1 is E^m-1=0,
44: and E-1 is an isogeny; the connected image of the sum cannot be
45: a nontrivial subgroup of that finite kernel. The actual norm
46: A^m=B^n therefore requires n Q_0=0. For Q=G its central limit is
47: the nonzero fixed point of exact order p. Since p divides m and
48: gcd(m,n)=1, this equation is impossible. This proves the excluded
49: good-root norm for all three primitive cases directly. It does
50: not exclude the weighted partner Q=pG, whose marked limit is O.
51: 
52: For a reduced III or IV root, the deck fixes all component labels,
53: and m is prime to p. The affine component norm is
54: m[R]=n[Q] in Z/p. Its generated component subgroup is exactly
55: that generated by Q. Thus the weighted partner Q=pG cannot be
56: transitive on this reduced root. The other residues give the
57: nonreduced I0*, III*, or IV* fibres; their actual multiplicities
58: and the intersection obstruction P3K.19 exclude them from this
59: one-cap sphere sector. No root has been replaced by an unmarked
60: smooth fibre in these exclusions.
61: 
62: ## OA.2. Resolved roots, lines and commuting affine norms
63: 
64: For III use its actual minimal equation
65: 
66:     y^2=x^3+q a(q)x+q^2 b(q),  a(0) nonzero.
67: 
68: For m=4l+1 substitute q=z^m, x=z^(2l)X, y=z^(3l)Y:
69: 
70:     Y^2=X^3+z a(z^m)X+z^(2l+2)b(z^m).
71: 
72: The A1 resolution gives the reduced III fibre. Its two component
73: labels are fixed by the deck
74: D(X,Y,z)=(zeta_m^(-2l)X,zeta_m^(-3l)Y,zeta_m z).
75: For m=4l the same substitution gives the smooth family
76: 
77:     Y^2=X^3+a(z^m)X+z^(2l)b(z^m),
78: 
79: whose central deck is (X,Y)->(-X,iY).
80: 
81: For IV the actual equation is
82: 
83:     y^2=x^3+q^2 a(q)x+q^2 b(q),  b(0) nonzero.
84: 
85: For m=3l+1 the substitution with k=l gives
86: 
87:     Y^2=X^3+z^(2l+2)a(z^m)X+z^2 b(z^m).
88: 
89: Resolve its A2 point. In X=zX1,Y=zY1 the nonidentity components
90: are Y1=+/-sqrt(b(0)), and the deck character on Y1 is
91: zeta_m^(-(3l+1))=1. Thus all three labels are fixed. For m=3l
92: the same substitution is good, with equation
93: 
94:     Y^2=X^3+z^(2l)a(z^m)X+b(z^m)
95: 
96: and central deck (X,Y)->(zeta_3 X,Y).
97: 
98: The marked source G meets the nonidentity component of III or IV.
99: Its limit on the good root is the corresponding nonzero fixed
100: point of order p. This follows by the actual coordinate
101: w=-X/Y at O: deck-invariant sections with limit O have
102: w(z)=z^k c(z^r), and substitution in the original x,y coordinates
103: lands on the original identity component. The converse follows
104: from the same inverse chart. Thus the component marking used
105: above is the one carried by the given Weierstrass map.
106: 
107: At a reduced root, N=O(aG'-O) has degree zero on every component.
108: Pull back M to the resolved fibre product and carry it through
109: the actual relative contractions: each contracted curve has
110: degree zero. The descended line and N have the same generic
111: Picard point; their vertical comparison is an integral multiple
112: of the whole fibre, because the reduced III/IV intersection
113: matrix has precisely that primitive kernel. The base monomial
114: with that exponent retains the deck character. This supplies
115: the extending actual suspension map B0 on N.
116: 
117: For good IV roots the full correspondence and its line divisor
118: are IV.2 in notes/part3_iv_caps_working.md. Its three fixed points
119: are blown up, the quotient is taken, and its central (-1)-curve
120: is contracted. In that actual diagram,
121: 
122:     beta^*N = pi^*alpha^*M + E_(aG) - E_O.
123: 
124: The following gives the good III correspondence explicitly.
125: On the minimal fourth root the central elliptic curve has two
126: order-four fixed points O,P and one orbit {R1,R2} with stabilizer
127: of order two. The local weights at O,P are (1,1) for mu4, and
128: those at R1,R2 are (1,1) for mu2. Blow up these four points by
129: beta. In the quotient pi, the images D0,D1 of the first two
130: exceptional curves have square -4, the image D2 of the latter
131: pair has square -2, and the central image C has square -1.
132: Indeed pi^*D0=4E0, pi^*D1=4E1,
133: pi^*D2=2(E_R1+E_R2), and pi^*C is the proper central elliptic
134: curve of square -4. The quotient fibre is
135: 
136:     4C + D0 + D1 + 2D2.
137: 
138: Contract C. The three remaining squares become -3,-3,-1,
139: and their pairwise intersections are one. Contract D2 next.
140: The remaining two curves have square -2 and intersection two;
141: the displayed invariant Weierstrass map identifies their
142: tangency as the original III fibre. These are the actual
143: relative-minimal contractions. The strict marked sections pass
144: through D0,D1 away from both contracted curves. Therefore
145: 
146:     beta^*N = pi^*alpha^*M + E_P - E_O.                 (OA.1)
147: 
148: This identity is a divisor identity, including its exceptional
149: coefficients. The root limit of Q=pG is O. Its source translation
150: is the identity on the central elliptic curve, fixes all four
151: centres, lifts to their blowup, and preserves the correction in
152: (OA.1). The quotient and contractions consequently carry the
153: actual extending B0 and its deck commutation to N. For m=rl,
154: pull back the whole diagram through the further base change
155: and resolve that fibre product. This retains the divisor identity
156: and the maps; it does not discard the ramification characters.
157: 
158: The central class of N in a good root is nonzero p-torsion.
159: The connecting map from fixed elliptic points to
160: Lambda/(D-1)Lambda sends [v] to [(D-1)v]; it identifies this
161: class with the primitive p-torsion Euler class of the circle
162: line on the cap. A common deck character contributes the flat
163: line from the free suspension circle and does not remove it.
164: 
165: Now take gcd(m,n)=1. At a reduced root multiplication by m
166: is invertible on the additive identity group and on Z/p; at
167: a good root use its inverse branch at O. This gives the unique
168: marked analytic R with mR=Q', and D-invariance follows from
169: uniqueness. The source map t_(-nR)D has mth power t_(-nQ').
170: Lift t_(-R) first to F on N. The exact chord/vertical comparison
171: and deck eigenspace averaging give DFD^(-1)=chi F, chi^m=1.
172: The actual descended lift is B0=beta(q)F^m, with beta a base
173: unit: deck commutation forces beta to be invariant. Thus F,D
174: commute with B0 and
175: 
176:     (F^n D)^m / B0^n = chi^(n m(m-1)/2) beta(q)^(-n).
177: 
178: Divide by an invariant analytic mth root of this unit. The
179: result A0 satisfies A0^m=B0^n and [A0,B0]=1, including the
180: displayed character factor for the even good-root orders.
181: 
182: Put A_r=r^n A0 and B_lambda=r^m B0. If nu+mv=1, then
183: H=A_r^u B_lambda^v=r H0 satisfies H^m=B_lambda and H^n=A_r.
184: A smaller compact source tube makes H strictly contracting for
185: small |r|. Its norm shell gives the actual smooth proper cap
186: N^times/<H> and proves that delta is free there. For outward
187: h=H^(-1), the one-A cut is
188: 
189:     q -> -n h,  gamma_out -> m h,
190: 
191: so the marked killed meridian is m q+n gamma_out. The ordinary
192: to cap cochain has scalar variation n(c-c0)s delta/m. At
193: c->c+m the inverse closing is (t-t_c)^(-n); the positive
194: deck is its inverse. As in SC.4, the cusp comparison is by
195: actual B iterates, bounded source translations and invertible
196: toric units. Thus these give holomorphic compact families on
197: the punctured r-disc, not only isolated smooth caps.
198: 
199: ## OA.3. Every integral source and boundary map
200: 
201: The full certificates are
202: code/part3/ordinary_additive_boundary.py/.json/.out, executed PASS.
203: They use only the actual NS Gram and negative-translation matrices
204: of P3K.22. Fourfold independently supplied the primitive and
205: weighted complement calculations in
206: code/part3_fourfold/primitive_additive_complements.* and
207: weighted_additive_complements.*. The code retains all chain maps,
208: Smith representatives, component columns, and marked fibre traces.
209: 
210: Let L be the ten-by-p matrix of all components of the removed
211: reduced III/IV fibre, and B the unimodular source Gram matrix.
212: The actual source-complement chain model is
213: 
214:     C4=Z -> C3=Z -> C2=NS -> C1=Z^p -> C0=Z,
215:     d4=1, d3=0, d2=L^t B, d1=0.
216: 
217: The boundary model has C3=Z, C2=C1=Z^p, C0=Z and sole
218: nonzero differential d2=L^t B L. Its inclusion is identity
219: in degrees zero, one and three, and L in degree two.
220: The source translation T=t_(-bG) acts by T on NS and by
221: the actual permutation P on components, TL=LP. On dual
222: meridians it acts by P^(-t). The code forms both integral
223: mapping cones and their inclusion. The even source and relative
224: complexes have free groups, so these displayed maps compute
225: the actual relative Wang maps without an omitted torsion choice.
226: 
227: The complement V is simply connected. It has a section, so its
228: fundamental group is a quotient of the abelian elliptic lattice.
229: The component intersection map L^tB is surjective: replace its
230: rows by F and the p-1 root-component rows, which are selected
231: rows of the unimodular B. Therefore H1(V)=0, proving pi1(V)=0.
232: Equivalently the remaining nodal vanishing cycles span the full
233: lattice: their product has trace zero at III or -1 at IV;
234: trace modulo the square of the spanning index gives d^2|2 or
235: d^2|3, hence d=1. The exact matrices supply the integral proof
236: without requiring that alternative trace argument.
237: 
238: In the primitive case b=1, the exterior W and its boundary E have
239: 
240:     H1(W)=H2(W)=H3(W)=Z, H4(W)=0,
